				\begin{document}$$d_{\textrm{w}}$$\end{document} . This is made possible by the unrestricted accessibility when measuring with calipers according to ISO 17677-1 [ 2 ]. The good accessibility results from the fact that no material residues remain on the spot welds after DT, as it is often the case with other test methods, especially with ductile fracture behavior. A consideration of the test rates  n  proposed in ISO 17653 [ 7 ] shows that they are significantly higher than those in ISO 18338 [ 12 ] for torsion test at room temperature of metallic materials as shown in Table  1  and Eq.  1 . It also gives the advice that the test rate  n  should be“as uniform as possibl” [ 12 ], which does not include a clear description of the test rate tolerance. However, there is a paucity of published studies that provide a scientific rationale for higher test rates. No investigations have been carried out to date that examine the influence of the test rate in the context of different sheet thicknesses for torsion tests of spot welds. This article aims to address this discrepancy between both ISO standards by conducting a study to investigate the influence of the test rate on the results of the torsion test of spot-welded joints. Two representative steel grades with different elongation and deformation behaviors are considered: one representing ductile steel, and the other representing advanced high-strength steel. This allows a differentiated consideration of the effects of different test speeds and sheet thickness combinations on the fracture behavior, the energy absorption, and the strain rate dependencies. 1 n ( ISO 17653 ) n ( ISO 18338 ) = 361.4 ... 3614 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
